  "narrative": "The new independent validation for H9 decisively supports the MSI-high/WRN dependency association. Because H9 was registered with direction -1, the signed validation estimate +0.802 means the raw MSI-high minus non-MSI-high contrast on dependency_WRN is about -0.802, i.e. MSI-high lines show a much stronger WRN dependency. The adjusted independent 95% interval, approximately -0.838 to -0.753 after sign conversion, is extremely narrow relative to the dependency-score scale and far exceeds the 10% relative clinical-significance benchmark on the observed outcome scale. The cell sizes are adequate, including 124 MSI-high observations in the validation subset. I therefore accept H9 and keep the investigation active only to assess robustness across lineage and technical library contexts. Current strongest dataset-specific conclusions are: SMARCA4 loss is associated with very strong SMARCA2 dependency, including in high-Cas9 screens; MSI-high is associated with very strong WRN dependency and this has now been independently validated; and KEAP1 loss is associated with markedly weaker NFE2L2 dependency, with the originally registered KEAP1 direction rejected and the reversed claim validated. Subgroup robustness tests also support the KEAP1/NFE2L2 association in STK11-loss, KRAS-wild-type, and adenocarcinoma contexts, and support WRN/MSI-high and SMARCA4/SMARCA2 in high-Cas9 screens. Interaction tests to date do not show large effect modification, although their intervals do not exclude modest or clinically possible differences; they are unresolved rather than proof of no interaction. Several genotype hypotheses are negative or insufficiently supported: BRAF-V600E with BRAF dependency, KRAS mutation with RIT1 dependency, MYC amplification with POLR2A or DHX9 dependency, ERBB2 amplification with ERBB2 dependency, and ARID1A loss with the expected direction of SMARCA2 dependency were not supported. EGFR amplification remains borderline and unresolved, and the pending broad MYC/RPL11, MDM2/CDKN2A, USP7/TP53, and conditional ARID1A comparisons still need results. All accepted findings should be described as associations in this supplied dataset, with internal robustness but not causality or external generalizability. The most informative next steps are to validate the SMARCA4/SMARCA2 finding independently and to test whether the accepted large effects are stable across library and biological subgroups. I also prioritize the already registered broad mechanistic comparisons because they could identify additional meaningful predictors or context-dependent patterns without diverting effort from the established large effects. Research markers/signatures are not prioritized because they have no assigned biological role and would mostly increase multiplicity unless needed as negative controls.",
