lly convincing and biologically/clinically meaningful on the observed dependency-score scale because the effect is large relative to the MDM2 dependency mean and well above the 10% relative-difference threshold. I accept H53 as a dataset-specific association but keep it active because it is a data-dependent reversal and lacks independent validation.\n\nThe current strongest dataset-supported conclusions are: (1) SMARCA4 loss is associated with very strong SMARCA2 dependency, with this main effect now independently validated; (2) MSI-high status is associated with very strong WRN dependency, already independently validated; and (3) KEAP1 loss is associated with markedly weaker NFE2L2 dependency, with the originally registered opposite direction rejected and the reversed claim independently supported. These associations remain dataset-specific and should not be interpreted as causal or externally generalizable. Subgroup analyses suggest broad robustness in high-Cas9, adenocarcinoma, and context-conditional subsets, while library and several genetic interactions appear small or underpowered.\n\nImportant negative or unsupported comparisons include BRAF-V600E/BRAF dependency in the registered direction, KRAS-mutant/RIT1 dependency, MYC-amplification effects on POLR2A or DHX9, ERBB2-amplification/ERBB2 dependency, and an independent ARID1A-loss effect on SMARCA2 dependency when SMARCA4-loss lines are excluded. EGFR amplification remains unresolved: the PIK3CA-wildtype subgroup shows a statistically significant and potentially meaningful point estimate, but its lower confidence bound approaches non-meaningful effects and the finding arose after multiple context tests, so it should not be upgraded without additional independent evidence.\n\nThe most informative next analysis is independent validation of H53, the reversed CDKN2A-loss/stronger-MDM2-dependency association within TP53-wildtype lines, because it is a new large discovery with no validation. If validation capacity remains, EGFR amplification in PIK3CA-wildtype context is the next priority. For already validated large associations, I would avoid further main-effect validation and focus on prespecified subgroup or technical robustness only where there is a specific scientific reason.",
